{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact head 0a5864b175b11f60f95f450e049ebbd6289ccd85, sole artifact notes/2026-10-01-artroom-plan.md.\n\nArchitecture conclusion: the revised durable landing operation, parallel preparation, immutable generations and published-prefix verification resolve most of the first review. One publication race still needs a defined ordering rule.\nSecurity conclusion: explicit checker identities, old-policy administration, separated runners, canonical envelopes and bounded evaluation are substantial improvements. Complete check-input coverage and admission-time revocation remain required.\nSimplification conclusion: the complete scope remains intact. The vertical slice and later staging provide a sensible implementation order. Neither estimate-driven scope cuts nor my former staffing recommendation are approval conditions (assert f92ef928). Planner may choose lane capacity as progress permits.\n\nThe first review's approval-reuse explanation, secret-publication limitation, policy-runtime bounds, async API states, capacity transparency and evidence qualifications are addressed at plan level. No request to reduce functionality remains.\n\nThree targeted corrections are required before plan approval:\n\n1. P1 — Define the publication linearization point, including changes during the push (lines 543–575, 579–609).\nThe publisher re-reads generation, lease, policy and evidence before starting an awaited Git push. While the push is in flight, a release, newer proposal, changes-requested review or key/role revocation can be accepted by the Room. main can still equal expectedMain, so Git's ref lease permits the old integration to land. Re-checking after the push discovers the problem after publication; it cannot prevent it.\nSpecify a durable reservation/order for publishing: before external push, atomically record the validated operation and reserve the relevant state. Mutations affecting its authorization or evidence must either precede that reservation and invalidate it, or follow its confirmed/reconciled outcome. Alternatively define publication as authorized irrevocably at reservation, and make that ordering visible to callers. A fixed publication lease must also fence the external writer; a SQLite counter alone does not stop a timed-out sandbox from completing an older push.\nRecovery must reconcile an unresolved publishing operation before any later operation can update main. Seeing expectedMain immediately after a timeout does not prove that an in-flight request can never complete. State how the old writer is ended/fenced or how retrying the same pinned integration remains safe. Add acceptance cases that pause the push itself, then submit release, proposal, objecting review and revocation, plus a delayed successful push after timeout. This is the remaining part of original finding P1.2, not a request to implement the landing engine in this note.\n\n2. P1 — Check input equivalence must include tests and all files the runner can read (lines 405–414, 500–508).\nThe Tests example declares inputs [src/**, package*.json], but npm test can also read tests/**, fixtures, test configuration and scripts. A new failing test outside src leaves the declared input hash unchanged, so the passing result could be carried without executing the new test. An exact checkout and trusted signer do not repair an incomplete input set.\nUse the complete repository tree plus checker configuration and pinned runner environment as the safe default. If scoped check reuse stays in the API, require a documented complete input contract or enforce that the runner cannot observe omitted inputs. Include global inputs and test sources in the example; do not allow repository-controlled declarations to narrow a required check unsafely. Add a changed-test/unchanged-source acceptance case. The feature remains in full scope.\n\n3. P1 — Revocation is decided at admission, not claimed signing time (lines 613–636).\nThe signed envelope has no trusted signing-time proof. A revoked key can claim that a newly submitted act was signed earlier, or submit a previously signed privileged act after revocation. Replace \"refuses acts signed after it\" with a rule that new admissions require currently active membership, key and delegation authority at the sequenced admission point. A byte-identical idempotent replay may return its original receipt without creating a new act. State whether old evidence from a revoked reviewer remains valid or is invalidated; that is an explicit policy decision. Recheck/fence authorization of pending land operations using the ordering in item 1. Add pre-signed-act-after-revocation and expired-delegation cases.\n\nNonblocking matters for lane 0 and implementation review:\n- A sole initial admin can otherwise be unable to approve their own policy change under the default no-self-review rule. Define an explicit, auditable bootstrap/recovery path instead of relying on a dummy second identity.\n- The workspace example returns an operation but immediately refers to its remote. Show waiting for ready and handling failure when the contract is finalized. Validate and safely pass URLs and immutable object IDs in runner commands.\n- Serial publication with rebuilding on every main move is correct but may waste preparations. Measure it; batching can be added under the same safety rules without changing scope.\n\nValidation: read all of revision 2 and compared it with exact revision 1, the original review and subsequent scope direction. The listed estimates now sum to 32 days. This is a plan review, with no application tests or deployment. The remaining findings are reasoned from the documented state machine and API examples. No source files changed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fa712902de4374e126f7eb7d4acdabc5fce47f07","binding":"assigned","head":"0a5864b175b11f60f95f450e049ebbd6289ccd85","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736